  "conclusions": [
    "Grimoire demonstrated its clearest benefit on broad, distributed, and cross-layer investigations.",
    "Grimoire used the least processed input on all four directly comparable Codex tasks without reducing answer depth.",
    "Most Grimoire token savings came from less repeated cached context, not dramatically less fresh source evidence.",
    "Grimoire is not necessarily beneficial for narrow tasks where ownership and relevant control flow are cheap to find directly.",
    "The first LevelDB result was affected by model trajectory, but the second 19-call run shows the excessive investigation pattern was not merely a one-off outlier.",
    "Retrieval quality and agent stopping behavior must be measured separately.",
    "The primary LevelDB Grimoire run found correct evidence early and used the least fresh input; the end-to-end inefficiency came from repeated post-retrieval processing.",
    "Plain remained fastest overall and perfect in quality; Grimoire provided the best broad-task context efficiency and matched Plain grounding reliability; CBM prepared cheaply but produced the only invalid grounded answer.",
    "Future evaluation should stratify narrow and broad tasks and use repeated seeds before attributing results solely to model variance.",
    "The completed narrow-task path reduced the deterministic session-backed LevelDB discovery response by 89.7%, returned four nodes and four retrieval hits with zero source ranges, and added explicit assessment/stopping guidance; agent-trajectory improvement remains to be measured.",
    "The accepted post-mitigation LevelDB rerun matched Plain at 13 API calls, used 767,025 total tokens, and reduced the primary Grimoire result by 36.3%; it lost one quality point because its test plan omitted an explicit close-while-paused case."
  ],
